# Replication package

**Fiscal Capacity in the Digital Age: What Survives Identification? Evidence from 145 Countries**
(*Economies*, manuscript economies-4672368)

Package version 1.0.1 (11 November 2026) — master dataset version **16 October 2026** (`v2026-10-16`) — DOI of version 1.0.0: **10.5281/zenodo.23483609** (Zenodo assigns a new DOI to each version; the concept DOI resolves to the latest).

### What is new in version 1.0.1

* New scripts added during the revision of the article: `41b_did_late_adopters.py`, `41c_b2c_share.py`, `52_sample_composition.py`, `53_lag_sensitivity.py`, `54_weak_iv.py`, `55_democracy_resources.py`, `56_did_cohorts.py`, `57_mechanism_trends.py` and `58_osi_validation.py`; `49_heterogeneity.py` now also reports unadjusted p-values. The master dataset and all earlier estimates are unchanged.
* **Numbering.** In the published article the appendices are *Supplementary Materials S1–S16* (A → S1, B → S2, …, P → S16) and the supplementary tables and figures are numbered consecutively (Tables S1–S85, Figures S1–S19). The output files keep the internal names used by the code (`A1.csv` … `P5.csv`, `Table1.csv` … `Table11.csv`, `Figure1.png` … `Figure4.png`). **`docs/numbering_map.csv` gives, for every published table and figure, the corresponding output file and its caption.** References to appendices, tables and figures in this README and in the code use the internal names. Note that the order of the main-text tables and figures changed in the article: `Table7.csv` → Table 8, `Table8.csv` → Table 9, `Table9.csv` → Table 11, `Table10.csv` → Table 7, `Table11.csv` → Table 10, `Figure1.png` → Figure 2 and `Figure2.png` → Figure 1.
* Tables S19–S21 (internal A18–A20), S52–S54 (H5–H7), S62–S63 (J5–J6) and S68 (L3) are assembled from the JSON files written by the new scripts in `output/results/`; `docs/numbering_map.csv` names the file and key of each.

This package contains the data, the Python code and the instructions needed to reproduce **every table and figure** of
the article and of its Supplementary Material. All estimates are computed from **one dated master dataset**
(`data/panel_master_v2026-10-16.csv`), which is itself built by code from public source extracts and from
hand-collected data documented below.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## 1. Quick start

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt        # Python 3.11 or later (tested with Python 3.13)
python run_all.py                      # builds the master dataset, then runs every analysis
```

Outputs:

| Folder | Content |
|---|---|
| `output/tables/` | one CSV file per table (`Table1.csv` … `Table11.csv`, `A1.csv` … `P5.csv`), with notes in `*_note.txt` |
| `output/figures/` | one PNG file per figure (`Figure1.png` … `Figure4.png`, `A1.png` … `P1.png`) |
| `output/results/` | full estimation results in JSON (coefficients, standard errors, p-values, sample sizes, test statistics) |
| `output/run_all.log` | log of the complete run |

Run time: about 55 minutes on a 2-core cloud machine, most of it in the nested cross-validation of the
machine-learning comparison (`31_ml.py`), in the repeated cross-fitting of double machine learning (`33_dml_cf.py`) and in the wild and pairs bootstraps of `47_inference.py` and in the repeated sample splitting of `49_heterogeneity.py`.
Every random procedure uses a fixed seed, so a complete run reproduces the published numbers exactly (verified by
two complete runs from an empty output folder).

## 2. Data

### 2.1 Public sources (`data/raw/`)

The raw files are filtered extracts (only the indicators used) of the full downloads made on **29 September 2026**.
`code/00_extract_raw.py` recreates them from the full downloads (not shipped because of their size; place them in
`data/source_downloads/` and run `python code/00_extract_raw.py`).

| Extract | Provider and dataset | Indicators | Licence |
|---|---|---|---|
| `IMF_WORLD_extract.csv` | IMF World Revenue Longitudinal Data (WoRLD), via World Bank Data360 | tax revenue, total revenue, social contributions, grants and tax components (% of GDP) | CC BY 4.0 |
| `UNCTAD_DE_extract.csv` | UNCTAD digitally-deliverable services trade, via Data360 | imports and exports, US$ (2010–2023; single vintage) | CC BY 4.0 |
| `WB_KNOMAD_extract.csv` | World Bank KNOMAD | remittance inflows; bilateral migrant stocks | CC BY 4.0 |
| `EIU_DI_extract.csv` | EIU Democracy Index, via Data360 | overall index (national values) | as per provider |
| `RWB_PFI_extract.csv` | Reporters Without Borders, World Press Freedom Index, via Data360 | overall **score** (the rank series is discarded) | as per provider |
| `ITU_ICT_extract.csv` | ITU ICT Regulatory Tracker, via Data360 | overall score | as per provider |
| `WB_INFECDB_extract.csv` | World Bank Informal Economy Database | MIMIC, DGE, self-employment, informal employment and other measures | CC BY 4.0 |
| `PCH_IXP_extract.csv` | Packet Clearing House, via Data360 | number of Internet exchange points | as per provider |
| `UN_EGDI_extract.csv` | UN E-Government Survey, via Data360 | Online Services Index | as per provider |
| `WB_GTMI_extract.csv` | World Bank GovTech Maturity Index 2022 | items I-7, I-20, I-21, I-22 (tax administration) | CC BY 4.0 |
| `WB_ES_extract.csv` | World Bank Enterprise Surveys indicators, via Data360 (dataset WB_ES; downloaded 18 October 2026; built by `code/00c_extract_es.py`) | firm shares by detailed sector (standard surveys 2006–2026) and by firm group (Business Ready surveys 2022–2026): website use, electronic sales, tax visits, registration, e-filing, tax perceptions and firm composition | CC BY 4.0 |
| `WDI_extract.csv`, `WDI_country_metadata.csv` | World Bank World Development Indicators / Gender Statistics | GDP (current US$), GDP per capita (PPP, constant US$), population, inflation, agricultural and vulnerable/self-employment, urbanisation, Internet users, fixed broadband; regions | CC BY 4.0 |

### 2.2 Hand-collected data (`data/handcollected/`)

| File | Content | Sources |
|---|---|---|
| `digital_vat_adoption_dates.csv` | effective date of VAT/GST on business-to-consumer digital services supplied by non-residents, 145 countries, with confidence rating and source URL | tax-authority documents, VATCalc, Avalara, KPMG/EY/PwC alerts, EUR-Lex |
| `vat_standard_rates.csv` | standard VAT/GST rate, 145 countries × 2005–2020, with notes and source URL | KPMG and PwC tax guides, OECD *Consumption Tax Trends*, national legislation |
| `submarine_cables.csv` | international submarine cables landing in each coastal country with ready-for-service year | TeleGeography Submarine Cable Map (country data, accessed October 2026) |
| `eu_vat_compliance_gap.csv` | VAT compliance gap (% of VTTL), EU-27 and UK, 2013–2020, with edition and table of the report used | European Commission VAT gap reports, 2019–2023 editions |

### 2.3 Reference data (`data/reference/`)

| File | Content | Source |
|---|---|---|
| `country_coordinates.csv` | latitude and longitude of the capital city of each economy, used for the Conley spatial standard errors | GeoNames, via the `geonamescache` package 3.0.2 (CC BY 4.0); built by `code/00b_coordinates.py` |
| `original_submission_clusters.csv` | cluster membership reported in the original submission | original submission |

### 2.4 Master dataset

`code/01_build_master.py` merges all sources into `data/panel_master_v2026-10-16.csv` (country-year panel,
1990–2025, 221 economies; the estimation samples use 2005–2020) and `data/eu_vatgap_v2026-10-16.csv`.
It also constructs every derived variable (shares of GDP, logarithms, the regional leave-one-out and shift-share
instruments, the submarine-cable counts, the reform indicators, the VAT efficiency ratio, the interpolated Online
Services Index and the GovTech tax index). Lags are taken by calendar year. The codebook is `data/codebook.csv`.

