| `44_dynamics.py` | Appendix K; Section 4.4 (prediction P5) |
| `45_sensitivity.py` | Section 4.3 sensitivity analysis; Appendix L |
| `46_multiverse.py` | Figure 2 (specification curve over 480 specifications); Appendix M (Tables M1–M2); full list in `output/results/multiverse_all.csv` |
| `47_inference.py` | Appendix N (Tables N1–N5, Figure N1): wild cluster bootstrap, two-way, Driscoll–Kraay and Conley inference for the headline estimates; Kleibergen–Paap / Sanderson–Windmeijer F and Anderson–Rubin sets for every IV estimate; Holm, Benjamini–Hochberg and Romano–Wolf corrections; Section 4.7 |
| `48_firms.py` | Table 10 and Section 4.8 (Enterprise Survey evidence on observability); Appendix O (Tables O1–O5, Figures O1–O2) |
| `49_heterogeneity.py` | Table 11 (Section 6): pre-specified heterogeneity of the digital effect (best linear projection, ordered group effects with sup-t bands, power) and generic machine-learning inference (BLP, GATES, CLAN); Appendix P (Tables P1–P5, Figure P1) |
| `50_composite_tables.py` | Tables 1, 4 and 9 (assembled from the results above) |
| `51_policy.py` | Appendix E (Tables E1–E3, Figure E1): policy simulations based on the identified reform effect (Tables 5–6), family-wise intervals, difference-in-differences check, welfare value and break-even costs; upper bounds for administrative digitalisation; Section 7 |
| `41b_did_late_adopters.py` | Robustness of the reform estimates to late (2020) adopters; Table S53 (internal H6) |
| `41c_b2c_share.py` | Business-to-consumer share of digital imports (Supplementary Material S11) |
| `52_sample_composition.py` | Tables S17–S19 (internal A16–A18): variable availability, sample coverage and composition tests |
| `53_lag_sensitivity.py` | Tables S20–S21 (internal A19–A20): lag structure of the internal instruments |
| `54_weak_iv.py` | LIML and Fuller estimates of Section 4.2 |
| `55_democracy_resources.py` | Table S68 (internal L3): democracy, non-tax revenue and tax revenue |
| `56_did_cohorts.py` | Tables S52–S53 (internal H5–H6): adoption cohorts, pre-trends, leave-one-out |
| `57_mechanism_trends.py` | Table S54 (internal H7): robustness of the imports × reform interaction |
| `58_osi_validation.py` | Tables S62–S63 (internal J5–J6): validation of the Online Services Index |

`code/config.py` sets the paths and the dataset version; `code/common.py` contains the shared helpers; `code/inference_lib.py` contains the robust-inference routines (also used by `20_baseline.py`, `21a` and `21c` for the weak-instrument rows of Tables 2, A4 and A8).

## 4. Notes

* The difference-in-differences estimator of Callaway and Sant'Anna (2021), the Dumitrescu–Hurlin test with
  bootstrap p-values, the Anderson–Rubin confidence sets (including the subset test of Guggenberger et al., 2012), the wild
  bootstraps (WCR, WRE, wild AR), the Driscoll–Kraay, Thompson and Conley estimators, the Romano–Wolf step-down and the
  Cinelli–Hazlett bounds are implemented directly in the scripts; the Driscoll–Kraay estimator was checked against `linearmodels`; the Cinelli–Hazlett computations were validated against the `sensemakr` package.
* System GMM uses `pydynpd`; double machine learning and the causal forest use `econml`. Double machine learning is
  repeated over ten random sample splits and the median estimate is reported (Chernozhukov et al., 2018).
* Between runs, only the computation times in Table C2 change (they depend on the hardware); all estimates are bit-for-bit reproducible (the causal forest is run single-threaded for this reason).
* Order matters only once: `33_dml_cf.py` uses the cluster membership produced by `30_clustering.py`; `run_all.py`
  runs the scripts in numerical order.
* `data/reference/original_submission_clusters.csv` holds the cluster membership reported in the original submission;
  it is used only to measure the agreement (adjusted Rand index) between the original and the current typology.
* The Enterprise Surveys download reached the 1,048,575-row limit of the spreadsheet export, so the total-economy series
  after `WB_ES_T_REG4` are absent; `48_firms.py` uses the complete sector (`WB_ES_M_*`, `WB_ES_S_*`) and Business Ready
  (`WB_ES_T_BREADY_*`) series. Individual Enterprise Survey responses are not used (they require registration and cannot be
  redistributed).
* The sample of the revenue analyses ends in 2019–2020 because the IMF WoRLD series end there for most countries.

